##Day 8: Supervised Machine Learning: Tree-Based Models, KNN, SVM & Ensembles##

1. Decision Trees

Idea: Split the data with a series of if/else questions on features until each group (leaf) is mostly one class (or has similar values).

Key terms: root node, internal node, branch, leaf node, depth.

How the best split is chosen

Gini impurity: Gini = 1 − Σ pᵢ² (0 = pure node). This is the default in scikit-learn.
Entropy: H = − Σ pᵢ log₂(pᵢ)
Information gain: the drop in impurity after a split. The tree picks the split with the highest gain.
Regression trees: split to minimize MSE (or variance) in each child, and predict the mean of the leaf.

Strengths: easy to interpret and visualize, needs no feature scaling, handles numerical and categorical data, and captures non-linear relationships.

Weaknesses: overfits very easily (an unrestricted tree memorizes the training data) and is unstable (small data changes can give a very different tree).

Controlling overfitting (pruning)

max_depth
min_samples_split
min_samples_leaf
max_leaf_nodes
ccp_alpha (cost-complexity pruning)
2. Ensemble Learning

Core idea: Many weak or moderate models combined give a better, more stable model than one model alone.

Bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating)
Train many models on random bootstrap samples (sampling with replacement).
Average the predictions (regression) or take a majority vote (classification).
Reduces variance.
Random Forest
Bagging of decision trees, plus random feature selection at each split, so the trees are less correlated.
Important parameters: n_estimators, max_depth, max_features, min_samples_leaf.
Feature importance (feature_importances_) shows which features matter most.
OOB (out-of-bag) score is a free validation estimate from the samples each tree didn’t see (oob_score=True).
Boosting
Train models sequentially, each one correcting the errors of the previous one.
Reduces bias (and some variance).
AdaBoost: increases the weight of misclassified samples.
Gradient Boosting: each new tree fits the residual errors (the gradient of the loss).
XGBoost / LightGBM / CatBoost: fast, regularized, widely used on tabular data (introduction only today).
Key parameters: n_estimators, learning_rate, max_depth. Lower learning rate needs more trees.
	Bagging / Random Forest	Boosting
Training	Parallel	Sequential
Main goal	Reduce variance	Reduce bias
Overfitting risk	Lower	Higher (needs tuning)
Sensitivity to noise	Low	Higher
3. K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)
Lazy learner: no real training. It stores the data and predicts by looking at the k closest points.
Classification: majority vote of the neighbors. Regression: average of the neighbors.
Distance metrics: Euclidean (default), Manhattan, Minkowski.
Choosing k:
Small k → low bias, high variance (overfitting)
Large k → high bias, low variance (underfitting)
Pick k using cross-validation (odd values avoid ties in binary problems)
Feature scaling is mandatory (use StandardScaler or MinMaxScaler), since distances are scale-sensitive.
Limitations: slow on large datasets at prediction time, and poor in high dimensions (the curse of dimensionality).
4. Support Vector Machines (SVM)
Finds the hyperplane that separates classes with the maximum margin.
Support vectors: the points closest to the boundary. Only these define it.
Hard margin vs soft margin: real data is noisy, so we allow some misclassification.
Parameter C:
Large C → narrow margin, fewer errors, risk of overfitting
Small C → wider margin, more errors allowed, risk of underfitting
Kernel trick: handle non-linear data by implicitly mapping it to a higher dimension.
linear, poly, rbf (most common), sigmoid
Parameter gamma (RBF): high gamma → very local, wiggly boundary (overfits). Low gamma → smoother boundary.
Scaling is required. SVR is the regression version.
5. Naive Bayes
Based on Bayes’ theorem: P(class | features) ∝ P(class) × Π P(featureᵢ | class)
“Naive” because it assumes features are independent given the class.
Variants: GaussianNB (continuous features), MultinomialNB (word counts, text), BernoulliNB (binary features).
Very fast, works well on small datasets and text classification (e.g., spam detection).
Laplace smoothing (alpha) avoids zero probabilities.
6. Hyperparameter Tuning
Parameters are learned from data (e.g., regression coefficients, tree splits). Hyperparameters are set by you before training (e.g., max_depth, k, C).
GridSearchCV: tries every combination in a grid, with cross-validation. It is exhaustive but slow.
RandomizedSearchCV: samples random combinations. It is faster and often nearly as good.
Pipelines (Pipeline) chain preprocessing and model, so scaling is fitted only on training folds. This prevents data leakage.
Tune using CV on the training set. Touch the test set only once, at the end.
7. Choosing a Model
Model	Needs scaling?	Interpretable?	Handles non-linearity?	Typical use
Linear / Logistic Regression	Yes (esp. regularized)	High	No	Baseline, simple relationships
Decision Tree	No	High	Yes	Explainable rules
Random Forest	No	Medium	Yes	Strong general-purpose model
Gradient Boosting / XGBoost	No	Low	Yes	Best accuracy on tabular data
KNN	Yes	Medium	Yes	Small datasets, quick baseline
SVM	Yes	Low	Yes (kernels)	Medium-sized, high-dimensional data
Naive Bayes	No	Medium	Limited	Text, fast baseline

Rule of thumb: Start with a simple baseline, then try Random Forest or Gradient Boosting, and compare with cross-validation.

8. Practical Implementation
python
import pandas as pd
from sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancer
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, cross_val_score, GridSearchCV
from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier
from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier, GradientBoostingClassifier
from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier
from sklearn.svm import SVC
from sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB
from sklearn.metrics import classification_report, confusion_matrix

# 1. Load data and split (stratify keeps class proportions)
X, y = load_breast_cancer(return_X_y=True)
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(
    X, y, test_size=0.2, stratify=y, random_state=42
)

# 2. Define models (scale-sensitive ones go in a pipeline)
models = {
    "Decision Tree": DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=4, random_state=42),
    "Random Forest": RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=200, random_state=42),
    "Gradient Boosting": GradientBoostingClassifier(random_state=42),
    "KNN": Pipeline([("scale", StandardScaler()), ("knn", KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5))]),
    "SVM (RBF)": Pipeline([("scale", StandardScaler()), ("svm", SVC(kernel="rbf", C=1.0))]),
    "Naive Bayes": GaussianNB(),
}

# 3. Compare train/test scores and 5-fold CV
results = []
for name, model in models.items():
    model.fit(X_train, y_train)
    cv = cross_val_score(model, X_train, y_train, cv=5, scoring="f1")
    results.append({
        "Model": name,
        "Train Acc": model.score(X_train, y_train),
        "Test Acc": model.score(X_test, y_test),
        "CV F1 (mean)": cv.mean(),
        "CV F1 (std)": cv.std(),
    })
print(pd.DataFrame(results).round(3).sort_values("CV F1 (mean)", ascending=False))

# 4. Hyperparameter tuning with GridSearchCV
param_grid = {
    "n_estimators": [100, 200],
    "max_depth": [None, 5, 10],
    "min_samples_leaf": [1, 3, 5],
}
grid = GridSearchCV(RandomForestClassifier(random_state=42),
                    param_grid, cv=5, scoring="f1", n_jobs=-1)
grid.fit(X_train, y_train)
print("Best params:", grid.best_params_)

# 5. Final evaluation on the untouched test set
best = grid.best_estimator_
y_pred = best.predict(X_test)
print(confusion_matrix(y_test, y_pred))
print(classification_report(y_test, y_pred))

# 6. Feature importance
importances = pd.Series(best.feature_importances_,
                        index=load_breast_cancer().feature_names)
print(importances.sort_values(ascending=False).head(5))

What to observe

An unrestricted Decision Tree has train accuracy near 100% but a lower test score. This is overfitting.
Random Forest usually closes that gap.
KNN and SVM perform poorly without scaling, so try removing the scaler and compare.
